    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country E.164 national-number length validation for owner telephone numbers.\n\n**Where the logic lives:** `TelephoneNormalizer` is the single source of truth used by both the bean-validation constraint (`@TelephoneValidation`, wired onto the generated `OwnerFieldsDto` via `x-field-extra-annotation` in `openapi.yml`) and the create/update mapper. So adding the rule there enforces it at the 400-returning validation boundary.\n\n**Change** (`TelephoneNormalizer.toE164`): after stripping separators, applying the `+61`-default rule, and the generic 8\u201315 total-digit check, it now also verifies the national number (digits after the country code) has the exact length the country requires:\n\n```java\nprivate static boolean hasValidNationalLength(String digits) {\n    if (digits.startsWith(\"61\")) return digits.length() - 2 == 9;   // +61 \u2192 9 national digits\n    if (digits.startsWith(\"1\"))  return digits.length() - 1 == 10;  // +1  \u2192 10 national digits\n    return true; // unknown country code: generic 8\u201315 rule only\n}\n```\n\nThe `61`/`1` prefixes don't overlap, so detection is unambiguous. Unknown country codes keep the prior generic behaviour, so nothing else regresses. When the national length is wrong, `toE164` returns `null`, the validator reports invalid, and the endpoint responds **400**.\n\nI also updated the `@TelephoneValidation` message and the Javadoc to describe the new per-country rule accurately.\n\n**Test result:** `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (posts `+61 123`, expects 400) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\nNote: this repo isn't a git repository, so I haven't committed anything \u2014 let me know if you'd like me to initialize git and commit.",
