        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new logic sits in the right unit \u2014 `TelephoneNormalizer` already owns all E.164 shaping (country-code assumption, digit stripping, the 8\u201315 total-digit rule), so per-country national-length validation belongs here rather than in the annotation or a new class. The extraction into a private static `hasValidNationalLength` helper, placed directly after its caller and gated in `toE164` right beside the existing length check, is the natural shape and reads consistently with the file.\n\nIt's additive and non-duplicative: `TelephoneValidation` continues to delegate to the normalizer via `isValid`, so the annotation change is limited to doc/message text and no validation logic is copied there. The inline literals (`\"61\"`, `\"1\"`, `9`, `10`) match the surrounding idiom, which already hard-codes `\"61\"`, `\"0\"`, `8`, and `15` inline \u2014 introducing a country-code table or config abstraction for two entries would be over-engineering relative to the existing altitude. The Javadoc additions follow the established `<li>`/`@param`/`@return` style. A maintainer of this codebase would find the placement and structure natural."
